{"steps": [{"id": 1, "description": "Confirm the user model's timezone attribute name and nullability by reading the users model, and resolve the open question about how the user argument exposes timezone before writing the helper.", "files": ["notifications/models (users model module)"]}, {"id": 2, "description": "Implement next_digest_time(user, now_utc) in the notifications digest module using zoneinfo: validate now_utc is aware, resolve the user's IANA zone with a UTC fallback for null, empty or invalid names, compute today's 09:00 local, advance one calendar day if it is not strictly after now_utc, and return an aware UTC datetime.", "files": ["notifications/digest (existing digest scheduling module)"]}, {"id": 3, "description": "Wire the scheduler's hourly dispatch to call next_digest_time for each recipient in place of the fixed 09:00 UTC time, leaving the rest of the dispatch mechanism unchanged.", "files": ["notifications/scheduler (existing digest scheduler module)"]}, {"id": 4, "description": "Add a log line or counter for fallback to UTC when the stored timezone is invalid, keeping the existing logger pattern of the notifications service and not adding a new metrics abstraction.", "files": ["notifications/digest (existing digest scheduling module)"]}, {"id": 5, "description": "Add unit tests covering the acceptance criteria, including the spring-forward DST case for America/New_York on 2024-03-10, the fall-back ambiguity, the already-passed-today rollover, and UTC fallback for invalid input, following the existing notifications test layout.", "files": ["notifications/tests (existing digest test module)"]}, {"id": 6, "description": "Update scheduler tests so the hourly run dispatches each user at their local 09:00 and does not send the digest twice for the same user on the same local day.", "files": ["notifications/tests (existing scheduler test module)"]}],
"risks": ["DST transitions can produce nonexistent or ambiguous local 09:00 times; mitigate by relying on zoneinfo with explicit fold handling and dedicated tests for spring-forward and fall-back dates.", "Invalid or null users.timezone values could silently send at the wrong time; mitigate with the UTC fallback, a logged warning, and a test for each invalid input class.", "Hourly scheduler may dispatch a user twice or miss a user if the window boundary falls exactly on 09:00 local; mitigate by using strictly-after comparison and adding a scheduler test for the boundary hour.", "Scheduler change alters live send timing for all recipients; mitigate by keeping the diff to the call site and confirming rollout behind existing feature toggles if available.", "Open question on deduplication is unresolved; mitigate by confirming with the team before merge and noting the assumption in the PR description."],
"test_plan": ["America/Los_Angeles with now_utc 2024-01-10T00:00:00Z returns 2024-01-10T17:00:00Z as an aware UTC datetime.", "Asia/Kolkata with now_utc 2024-01-10T04:00:00Z returns 2024-01-10T03:30:00Z.", "America/New_York on 2024-03-10 with now_utc before 09:00 local returns 09:00 local on that day expressed as UTC with the post-transition offset (DST case).", "America/New_York on 2024-03-10 with now_utc after 09:00 local returns 09:00 local on 2024-03-11 expressed as UTC.", "Fall-back day in America/New_York (2024-11-03) returns a single correct 09:00 local UTC instant, covering fold ambiguity.", "User whose local 09:00 today is exactly equal to now_utc returns the following day's 09:00, strictly after now_utc.", "User whose local 09:00 today has already passed returns the following calendar day's 09:00.", "User with timezone null returns 09:00 UTC and a result strictly after now_utc.", "User with empty string and with an invalid IANA name such as 'Mars/Olympus' returns 09:00 UTC and logs the fallback.", "Returned value is an aware datetime with tzinfo UTC, and the module imports zoneinfo and not pytz.", "Scheduler test: hourly run dispatches each user only at their local 09:00 and does not dispatch twice on the same local day."]}

{"steps": [{"id": 1, "description": "Confirm the shape of the user record in the notifications service and how users.timezone is exposed, so the helper reads the same attribute the rest of the service uses without introducing a new accessor.", "files": ["notifications/models/user.py", "notifications/repositories/users.py"]}, {"id": 2, "description": "Add a next_digest_time(user, now_utc) helper in the existing digest scheduling module that resolves the user's timezone via zoneinfo.ZoneInfo, falls back to UTC on null, empty, or invalid names, builds the candidate 09:00 local datetime for now_utc's local date, and advances one calendar day when the candidate is not strictly after now_utc, returning the result as an aware UTC datetime.", "files": ["notifications/digest/scheduling.py"]}, {"id": 3, "description": "Wire the new helper into the existing hourly digest scheduler in place of the hard-coded 09:00 UTC computation, keeping the scheduler loop and digest content unchanged.", "files": ["notifications/digest/scheduler.py"]}, {"id": 4, "description": "Add unit tests for next_digest_time covering each acceptance criterion: the Los Angeles and Kolkata cases, the New York spring-forward day on 2024-03-10 before 09:00 local, the exact-boundary case returning the following day, and null, empty, and unrecognised timezone fallback to UTC, with an assertion that every result has tzinfo UTC.", "files": ["notifications/tests/test_digest_scheduling.py"]}, {"id": 5, "description": "Add scheduler-level tests confirming that the hourly job selects users whose local 09:00 falls in the current window and does not double-send or skip a recipient across the DST transition hour.", "files": ["notifications/tests/test_digest_scheduler.py"]}],
"risks": ["Spring-forward gap handling: if a DST transition ever places 09:00 in a gap, zoneinfo's default fold behaviour could produce an unexpected instant; mitigate by confirming no supported zone has a gap at 09:00 and adding a test that documents the behaviour.", "Invalid or legacy timezone strings (such as abbreviations like PST) silently falling back to UTC could send mail at the wrong hour without alerting anyone; mitigate by logging a warning with the user id and raw value on fallback, pending the open question on reporting.", "Hourly scheduler boundary: if the scheduler's window does not align with the computed UTC instant, recipients could be skipped or sent twice; mitigate by making the scheduler query by next_digest_time falling within the current hour and covering it in tests.", "Switching from a fixed UTC send time changes delivery for all users at once; mitigate by noting the rollout in the PR description so growth can monitor open rates and volume per hour.", "Missing dependency on tzdata in deployment images could make ZoneInfo lookups fail; mitigate by checking the base image for system tzdata or the tzdata package."],
"test_plan": ["AC1: next_digest_time for a user with America/Los_Angeles and now_utc 2024-03-01T12:00:00Z returns 2024-03-01T17:00:00Z.", "AC2: next_digest_time for a user with Asia/Kolkata and now_utc 2024-03-01T03:30:00Z returns the next 09:00 IST instant expressed as an aware UTC datetime, asserted against a precomputed value.", "AC3: next_digest_time for America/New_York on 2024-03-10 with now_utc before 09:00 EDT returns 09:00 EDT as UTC, not shifted by one hour, and the result matches the expected UTC offset of -04:00 local.", "AC4: next_digest_time when now_utc equals the local 09:00 instant returns 09:00 local on the following calendar day.", "AC5: next_digest_time for users with timezone null, empty string, and an unrecognised name such as 'Mars/Olympus' returns 09:00 UTC without raising, and a warning is logged for the invalid case.", "AC6: For every test case above, assert the returned datetime is timezone-aware with tzinfo equal to UTC.", "DST case (QA request): run next_digest_time across the 2024-03-10 and 2024-11-03 US transitions for America/New_York and America/Los_Angeles at several now_utc values around 09:00 local and confirm results advance exactly one day in local time.", "Scheduler test: simulate hourly ticks across a DST transition and assert each recipient receives exactly one digest per local day, with none skipped or duplicated.", "Regression: confirm the digest content and send path are unchanged by running the existing digest test suite."]}

{"steps": [{"id": 1, "description": "Confirm the existing timezone field on the user model and how it is loaded, then add a helper that validates an IANA timezone name and returns UTC when the value is null, empty, or invalid.", "files": ["api/models/user.py", "api/utils/timezone.py"]}, {"id": 2, "description": "Implement `next_digest_time(user, now_utc)` in the existing digest scheduling module using the standard library zone database, converting now_utc into the user's local zone, building the candidate 09:00 local instant for that date, and advancing one day when the candidate is not strictly after now_utc.", "files": ["api/services/digest_scheduler.py", "api/utils/timezone.py"]}, {"id": 3, "description": "Ensure the function returns an aware UTC datetime by normalising the localised 09:00 result to UTC, relying on the zone database for DST offsets so that transition days resolve to the correct EDT or PST offset rather than a shifted hour.", "files": ["api/services/digest_scheduler.py"]}, {"id": 4, "description": "Wire the new calculation into the hourly scheduler so that a user is selected for dispatch only when now_utc has reached the computed next_digest_time, and record the last sent date per user to prevent duplicate sends around DST boundaries.", "files": ["api/services/digest_scheduler.py", "api/tasks/digest_dispatch.py"]}, {"id": 5, "description": "Add unit tests covering each acceptance criterion and the DST and invalid-timezone edge cases, and confirm the notification service consumes the UTC timestamp without its own timezone logic.", "files": ["api/tests/services/test_digest_scheduler.py", "api/tests/utils/test_timezone.py", "notifications/src/digest/schedule.ts"]}],
"risks": ["DST transitions could cause a 09:00 local instant to be skipped or duplicated; mitigate by deriving the target from the zone database and tracking the last sent local date per user.", "Invalid or missing users.timezone values could raise exceptions in the scheduler loop; mitigate by validating in one helper and falling back to UTC with a logged warning.", "Hourly scheduler polling could dispatch late or twice if the comparison is off by the polling interval; mitigate with an idempotency check on the last sent date and a test with the scheduler stepping across the boundary.", "Open question 1 on fallback behaviour is unresolved; mitigate by isolating the fallback in one helper so the behaviour can change without touching callers.", "Changing the notification service's timezone handling could break existing consumers; mitigate by keeping the TypeScript change limited to reading the UTC timestamp."],
"test_plan": ["Verify next_digest_time returns 2024-03-01T17:00:00Z for America/Los_Angeles with now_utc 2024-03-01T12:00:00Z, and that the result is timezone-aware UTC (covers criterion 1).", "Verify next_digest_time returns 2024-03-01T03:30:00Z for Asia/Kolkata with now_utc 2024-03-01T04:00:00Z (covers criterion 2).", "Verify for America/New_York on the spring-forward day that a before-09:00 now_utc yields 09:00 EDT as the correct UTC instant, not a one-hour shift (covers criterion 3).", "Verify that when now_utc equals the exact 09:00 local instant, the result is the following day's 09:00 and is strictly greater than now_utc (covers criterion 4).", "Verify that null and empty users.timezone values compute 09:00 UTC on the next applicable day (covers criterion 5).", "Verify that an invalid IANA name does not raise, falls back to UTC, and logs a warning (covers criterion 6).", "Simulate the hourly scheduler across a DST transition and assert each user receives exactly one digest per local day with no skips or duplicates (covers risk around duplicate or skipped sends)."]}

{"steps": [{"id": 1, "description": "Confirm the existing user model exposes the `timezone` field and identify the current digest scheduling entry point in the notifications component before changing anything.", "files": ["notifications (digest scheduling module)", "users model in the Python API service"]}, {"id": 2, "description": "Add a pure function `next_digest_time(user, now_utc)` in the notifications digest scheduling module that resolves the user's IANA timezone using the standard zoneinfo library, falls back to UTC when the timezone is null, empty, or unrecognised, builds the 09:00 local wall-clock candidate for the local date of now_utc, converts it to UTC, and advances to the next day when the candidate is not strictly after now_utc.", "files": ["notifications (digest scheduling module)"]}, {"id": 3, "description": "Route the existing weekly digest scheduling logic through `next_digest_time` so that the stored or computed send time is derived per user instead of from the previous fixed offset or global schedule, keeping the change confined to the call site that computes the next send.", "files": ["notifications (digest scheduling module)", "notifications (digest dispatch entry point)"]}, {"id": 4, "description": "Verify the hourly scheduler still selects due digests correctly with the new per-user times by checking its window and idempotency logic, and adjust only the due-check comparison if it assumes whole-hour UTC send times.", "files": ["notifications (hourly scheduler module)"]}, {"id": 5, "description": "Add unit tests for `next_digest_time` covering each acceptance criterion, including the DST spring-forward date, the half-hour IST offset, the null and empty timezone fallback, and the exact-boundary case, and update any existing digest tests that assumed a fixed send time.", "files": ["notifications tests (digest scheduling test module)"]}],
"risks": ["Daylight saving transitions could produce non-existent or ambiguous local times; mitigate by relying on zoneinfo localisation and asserting the spring-forward case in tests.", "Invalid or unknown timezone strings could raise exceptions and halt scheduling for many users; mitigate by catching resolution errors, falling back to UTC, and logging the bad value.", "The hourly scheduler may miss or double-send digests if it assumes whole-hour UTC instants (e.g. Asia/Kolkata sends at 03:30Z); mitigate by reviewing its due-check window and confirming idempotency per user per day.", "Users will see a changed send time; mitigate by noting the change in release communication and confirming with product whether a one-time catch-up send is needed.", "The exact-boundary rule could cause a skipped day if now_utc equals the send instant on a retry; mitigate by the strict-after comparison and a test for that case."],
"test_plan": ["Given America/Los_Angeles and now_utc 2024-03-01T12:00:00Z, next_digest_time returns 2024-03-01T17:00:00Z.", "Given Asia/Kolkata and now_utc 2024-03-01T04:00:00Z, next_digest_time returns 2024-03-01T03:30:00Z when after now_utc, and 2024-03-02T03:30:00Z otherwise.", "Given America/New_York and now_utc on 2024-03-10, next_digest_time returns the 13:00Z instant corresponding to 09:00 EDT, not 14:00Z.", "Given a null or empty timezone, next_digest_time returns 09:00 UTC on the appropriate day.", "Given an invalid timezone string, next_digest_time falls back to 09:00 UTC and logs the invalid value without raising.", "Given now_utc exactly equal to a 09:00 local digest instant, next_digest_time returns the following day's instant, not now_utc.", "Every value returned by next_digest_time is a timezone-aware datetime in UTC.", "The hourly scheduler dispatches each user's digest exactly once per local day, including for half-hour offset zones and across the DST transition date."]}
